  "additional_concerns": [
    "Scope placement creates a material difference between candidates sharing the same question hash. With distributor status TRUE, offering FALSE and all other facts known and non-conflicting, node.142f34016b300f5c110eb249 and node.bc55be7e973a689d07f9e64b return FALSE, whereas node.2a6321b4ce90763bf0be564e returns OUT_OF_SCOPE. Node.97af37a2579f59b0ee43211f similarly places offering and promotional linkage in scope. Whether these conditions define an intentionally restricted assessment domain or should produce a negative answer remains unresolved; this is separate from the paragraph 2.3 claim.",
    "Node.6d6e7adc6faa9d94b74c08e5 and both offer-level candidates put promotional conditions in scope. A failed condition returns OUT_OF_SCOPE, and an unknown condition returns UNKNOWN even where the result body could otherwise be decisive. These consequences follow from the declared runtime and require agreement on the typed question's domain; they should not automatically be labelled substantive legal errors.",
    "The offer-level candidates establish product promotion at offer level and a non-discount gift at component level. Their supplied fact definitions do not explicitly require the same identified gift component to bear the promotional connection. If offer-level promotion necessarily covers that component, the aggregation may be appropriate; if a mixed offer can include an unrelated component, the formula may impose an unsupported prohibition. The attribution assumption remains unresolved.",
    "The circular's opening supports the SFC-authorised-fund context used by several candidates. This supports a contextual restriction, not a conclusion that paragraph 3.11 has no application outside that context. Conversely, candidates using broader distributor and investment-product descriptions need their assessments confined to the declared source scope.",
    "Paragraph 3.11 of the Code of Conduct and the answer to question 1 of the SFC's Code of Conduct frequently asked questions issued on 30 September 2010, identified in footnote 3, are not supplied as external dependencies. Their applicable texts are needed for independent verification of classifications and interpretive boundaries beyond the circular's express wording. No dependency IDs are available. These missing authorities are separate from paragraph 2.3's absence in the required-pair assessments.",
    "The supplied paragraph 9 language says returns or incentives may be gifts; it does not make that classification automatic. The candidates generally preserve this distinction through supplied gift classifications, but those classifications remain professional-judgment inputs rather than implemented determinations. A separate fee discount cannot exempt another gift component.",
    "A FALSE prohibition result or TRUE selected-gift-control compliance result does not grant general permission or establish compliance with paragraph 2.3, section 103 or other requirements. The compliance candidate's typed question is appropriately limited; interpreting its output as overall compliance would add an unsupported permission.",
    "Node.bc55be7e973a689d07f9e64b treats offer attribution as independent of funding and fulfilment. The circular's focus on offering supports not requiring actual payment or receipt, but does not resolve every third-party attribution arrangement. That attribution remains a supplied judgment, not an established conclusion for an unidentified arrangement.",
    "The supplied units do not establish a concrete effective interval. The candidates' temporal-basis declarations cannot by themselves establish applicability at a particular assessment time. This uncertainty does not undermine extraction of the express paragraph 2.3 duty.",
    "The prior E_STALE_REVIEW diagnostic identifies no offending checks or substantive disagreement. It supplies no reason to reverse any dimension or mark a candidate defective. These proposals retain every required pair and do not authorize pruning or certify overall executable correctness."
